---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Compounding — Why Time Matters More Than Rate";
const description =
  "How compounding works, why the last years contribute most, and why a small " +
  "difference in fees or an early withdrawal costs far more than it appears to.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Investing / Compounding">
  <h1>Compounding</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Compounding is earning returns on returns. Everyone knows the definition. The
    part worth internalising is how unevenly it arrives — almost all of the growth
    happens at the end, which is why interruptions are so expensive.
  </p>

  <h2>The arithmetic</h2>
  <div class="formula">Final value = P × (1 + r)^years</div>
  <p>
    ₹1,00,000 at 12% a year:
  </p>
  <div class="formula">10 years  →  ₹3,10,585     (gain ₹2.1 lakh)
20 years  →  ₹9,64,629     (gain ₹8.6 lakh)
30 years  →  ₹29,95,992    (gain ₹28.9 lakh)</div>
  <p class="lede">
    Doubling the period from 10 to 20 years does not double the gain — it
    quadruples it. From 20 to 30 years the value triples again. <strong>The final
    decade contributes more than the first two combined.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>Why this makes early years so valuable</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Money invested at 25 gets forty years of compounding; the same rupee invested
    at 40 gets twenty-five. At 12%, that difference is roughly five times the
    final value from an identical amount. Starting earlier beats investing more,
    within a wide range — which is an unusual situation in finance and worth
    acting on.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it quietly leaks</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>fees_compound_too</code><span class="meta">the big one</span></div>
      <p>A 1% higher expense ratio does not cost 1%. Over 30 years at 12% versus
        11%, an initial ₹1,00,000 ends at about ₹29.96 lakh versus ₹22.89 lakh —
        a gap of over ₹7 lakh, from one percentage point. The fee compounds
        against you exactly as the return compounds for you.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>interrupting_it</code><span class="meta">costliest mistake</span></div>
      <p>Withdrawing in year 20 of a 30-year plan does not cost you a third of the
        outcome. It costs the largest third, because the final decade is where
        most of the growth was going to happen.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>tax_on_the_way</code><span class="meta">drag</span></div>
      <p>Gains taxed annually compound on a smaller base each year than gains
        taxed once at the end. The same nominal return produces a different
        outcome depending on when tax is paid.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>inflation</code><span class="meta">the silent half</span></div>
      <p>Compounding works on the other side too. At 6%, prices roughly double
        every twelve years. A nominal return that looks impressive can be a real
        loss — see <a href="/tools/inflation-calculator/">the inflation
        calculator</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The rule of 72</h2>
  <div class="formula">Years to double ≈ 72 / annual return %</div>
  <p class="lede">
    At 12%, six years. At 8%, nine years. At 6%, twelve years. A useful mental
    check, accurate enough for rates in the single to low double digits.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest caveat</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Every illustration above assumes a constant rate, and nothing delivers that.
    Real equity returns arrive in bursts separated by long flat or negative
    stretches, and the sequence matters — a large fall early in a withdrawal phase
    does far more damage than the same fall later.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Compounding is real. Smooth compounding is a diagram. The
    <a href="/tools/sip-calculator/">SIP</a> and
    <a href="/tools/lumpsum-calculator/">lumpsum</a> calculators use the same
    simplification, and it is a simplification.
  </p>

